       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order. Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg/day. That is double the labeled maximum daily dose of 2,550 mg for immediate-release metformin, and the extended-release maximum is lower still (2,000 mg in the US). A dose that high raises the risk of lactic acidosis, and I can't enter it, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\nHere are some options that stay within the label:\n- **850 mg PO three times daily with meals.** This is 2,550 mg/day, the immediate-release maximum.\n- **1,000 mg PO twice daily with meals.** This is 2,000 mg/day, a common target dose.\n- **500 mg PO twice daily with meals.** This is a typical starting dose if the patient is new to metformin. You can titrate it up.\n\n1,700 mg may have been a typo. For example, it could have been meant as the total daily dose of 1,700 mg, or as 850 mg three times daily. Tell me the regimen you want and I'll enter it right away."
      }
